      "id": "clm_01M4TPW4C7Y2193QD6J26GP095",
      "statement": "Belshazzar was \"king\" of Babylon and the son of Nebuchadnezzar (Dan 5:1–2, 11, 18, 22).",
      "layer": "reconstruction",
      "passage": "Dan.5.1-Dan.5.22",
      "classification": "disputed",
      "reasoning": "Babylonian documents call him \"son of the king\", never king, and do not date by his years; he was Nabonidus's son. Conservative interpreters read \"king\" as functional co-regency (kingship entrusted to him) and \"father\" as ancestor or royal predecessor, possibly through a mother descended from Nebuchadnezzar (a proposal, e.g. Shea 1982, via Wikipedia). Critical scholars read both as historical errors of a later author.",
